                                          ABSTRACT


    CDRL: Catfish-Effect-Based Deep Reinforcement Learning for Energy-Efficient Joint
       Switching of RF Chains and Reflecting Elements in RIS-Aided 6G Networks
                                          by
                                    KE, BO-HAN


                                          July, 2025

ADVISOR(S): Dr. CHEN, YUH-SHYAN
DEPARTMENT: COMPUTER SCIENCE & INFORMATION ENGINEERING
MAJOR: HYBRID BEAMFORMING AND PHASE SHIFT OPTIMIZATION WITH ON-
OFF CONTROL OF RF CHAIN AND REFLECTING ELEMENT FOR ENERGY-
EFFICIENCY RIS-AID DOWNLINK COMMUNICATIONS
DEGREE: MASTER OF SCIENCE


     A reconfigurable intelligent surface (RIS) is an emerging communications technology that
enhances link performance by reflecting electromagnetic signals. To address the challenges of
high-power consumption and hybrid discrete-continuous control introduced by active/passive
RIS in millimeter-wave downlink MU-MISO systems. This thesis proposes a Catfish-effect-
based deep reinforcement learning framework (Catfish-effect-based DRL, CDRL) for
simultaneously optimizing BS and RIS element‐switch control and beamforming, ultimately
maximizing energy efficiency (EE). In this thesis, the CDRL framework comprises two agents:
the catfish agent and the main agent. The catfish agent diverts according to an energy-efficiency
threshold to focus on collecting high-reward experiences, and is paired with a larger discount
factor and a competitive reward mechanism. The main agent handles general scenarios and,
during the random intervention phase, the catfish agent injects its long-term knowledge into the
main agent, achieving experience-level knowledge transfer and improved sample efficiency.
This dual-agent, dual-replay design advances DRL-based EE optimization for RIS systems with
joint switching control, and effectively mitigates policy stagnation issues often observed in
single-agent settings. Simulation results demonstrate that CDRL outperforms baseline DRL,
the analytical method BPOF, and Teacher-Student frameworks in both active and passive RIS
scenarios. CDRL achieves significant power savings under rate constraints, validating the
feasibility of integrating the catfish effect with DRL for wireless resource allocation. This thesis
highlights a new direction for green communications in 6G, motivated by the synergy between
BS control strategies and active RISs to reduce energy consumption while maintaining
transmission quality.



Key words: Energy efficiency, hybrid beamforming, reconfigurable intelligent surface, Deep Learning.

