Chapter 1

Introduction

    To accommodate the massive transmission demands of 6G, millimeter-wave (mmWave)

massive multiple-input single-output/multiple-input multiple-output (Massive MISO/MIMO)
technology has become a popular research direction and practical strategy in this field [4]-
[5][7]-[14][16]-[17][19]-[20]. mmWave transmission enables increased spectrum utilization and

enhanced data rates. However, due to its high-frequency characteristics, it results in relatively
short propagation distances and severe path loss [10, 14]. MIMO technology effectively ad-
dresses these issues by supporting multiple users, improving signal strength, and extending

communication coverage. MIMO allows the multiple antennas at the base station to perform
beamforming, which significantly enhances the performance of wireless systems by enabling
directional signal transmission. This improves the signal-to-noise ratio (SNR), mitigates co-

channel interference, and increases network capacity [1]. Conventionally, MIMO precoding is
realized through a fully digital (FD) method, where each antenna element is equipped with a
dedicated radio frequency (RF) chain [23]. However, deploying an RF chain for every antenna

at the base station leads to high hardware cost and power consumption [13,14]. Therefore, hy-
brid beamforming (HBF) is commonly adopted under this architecture to achieve satisfactory
beamforming gains with reasonable hardware complexity and power consumption [10, 13, 14].

   Due to the susceptibility of millimeter-wave (mmWave) communications to environmental
blockages such as walls and trees, which cause severe penetration loss, reconfigurable intelli-
gent surfaces (RIS) have been proposed to assist hybrid beamforming (HBF) [14]. RIS is an


                                               1
emerging next-generation technology that controls the reflection of electromagnetic waves by
reconfiguring the electromagnetic properties of the surface. RIS can effectively improve signal

transmission efficiency [5,10,14], expand wireless network coverage, and enhance transmission
quality [4, 16, 18]. These reflective surfaces are strategically deployed between the transmitter
and receiver, providing users with an additional communication link and improving signal

strength [10]. However, this induces multiplicative fading, leading to limited capacity gains
for conventional RIS [9]. Specifically, the communication channel reflected via RIS experiences
two stages of propagation loss, meaning that the equivalent path loss of the transmitter-RIS-

receiver link is the product of the transmitter-to-RIS and RIS-to-receiver path losses [10].
In general, conventional RIS consists of a large number of passive elements, each capable of
independently adjusting the phase of the incident electromagnetic wave to redirect the sig-

nal toward the target receiver [18]. In contrast, active RIS is a new type of RIS that can
simultaneously adjust the phase shift and amplify the received signal, thereby mitigating sig-
nal attenuation and eliminating the double fading effect associated with multiplicative fading

[9]. This enables active RIS to provide better communication performance under weak signal
power conditions.
   Network energy saving is critical for environmental sustainability, reducing environmen-

tal impact (e.g., greenhouse gas emissions), and lowering operational costs [2]. In wireless
communications, although the use of beamforming techniques at the transmitter via MIMO
and RIS-assisted transmission brings several advantages as mentioned above, such consider-

ations often lead to high power consumption. Therefore, improving energy efficiency while
maintaining acceptable transmission performance has become a vital issue. Laselva et al.
evaluated the potential of each radio domain, where radio adaptation in the antenna domain

involves adjusting the number of antennas (or antenna ports) used for transmission by the
base transceiver station (BTS), which in turn affects the total transmit power [3]. This implies
that deactivating specific antenna elements can reduce energy consumption, and this concept

can also be extended to RIS. Since RIS requires power supply to enable effective signal re-



                                               2
flection, selectively activating and deactivating reflecting elements can optimize RIS energy
usage [9]. However, due to the large number of elements involved and the mutual interference

among multiple users and multiple RIS units, jointly adjusting the phase shifts and on-off
states becomes a complex challenge. Therefore, the proposed solution adopted in this thesis
will be presented in the following chapters.

   With the increasing structural and functional complexity of RIS, efficiently controlling a
large number of RIS elements—including phase adjustment, on-off switching, and even power
amplification—has become a major challenge, especially under multi-user scenarios, dynamic

channels, or imperfect channel state information [12, 17, 19, 20]. Traditional optimization
approaches, such as convex programming and heuristic methods, often struggle to balance
efficiency and accuracy in these high-dimensional and non-convex problems [11, 12, 17]. As a

result, machine learning (ML) is increasingly regarded as a promising tool for assisting resource
allocation in communication systems, particularly due to its ability to model unknown environ-
ments and complex dependencies. Among various ML techniques, deep reinforcement learn-

ing (DRL) exhibits strong advantages in handling high-dimensional and sequential decision-
making problems [19,20]. Compared with traditional methods, DRL is especially beneficial in
wireless systems with time-varying radio channels. By modeling the communication system

as a Markov Decision Process (MDP), DRL can solve complex optimization problems with-
out explicitly formulating system models such as channel or mobility models, instead learning
through interaction with the environment and observing reward signals [11, 15, 17, 20]. DRL

is particularly well-suited for hybrid action spaces involving both discrete actions (e.g., RIS
switching) and continuous actions (e.g., power amplification) [17, 19, 20]. These features make
DRL one of the most active research directions for intelligent RIS control.

   To address these issues, we propose a novel Catfish-effect-based deep reinforcement learning
algorithm (CDRL), as illustrated in Fig. 1. We design two task-oriented agents, where the
Catfish Agent provides differentiated experiences to effectively stimulate the Main Agent to

explore better policies, thereby avoiding local optima. In summary, the contributions of this



