        In [10], Chen et al. formulated a maximization problem based on passive and active

RIS systems. To mathematically address the multiple coupled optimization variables, the
Dinkelbach method and quadratic transformation were adopted, and alternating optimization
(AO) was applied to approximate the optimal solution, as illustrated in Fig. 4. In [11], Huang

et al. proposed a DRL-based algorithm, where the state st is input to the reinforcement learn-
ing actor network to generate the resulting action at . The environment then transitions to
the next state and returns a reward rt based on at , while the critic network updates the actor

policy using the tuple (st , at , rt , st+1 ), as shown in Fig. 5. In [21], Zheng et al. introduced a
teacher-student learning framework that leverages the teacher’s domain knowledge to improve
the robustness of the student’s learning. They developed a confidence-check mechanism for

identifying critical states, reward shaping for injecting teaching signals into the student net-
work, and prioritized experience replay to address the challenge of limited teacher data, as

