        Table 3.1 shows the advantages and limitations of several competitor models relative to
CDRL, including Actor-Critic [11], BPOF [10] and Teacher-Student [21]. The Actor-Critic [11]

architecture combines policy-gradient and value-based methods, supports single-step updates
and is suitable for continuous action spaces, learns through interaction with the environment
and therefore does not rely on labeled samples, and has low implementation complexity that

is easily extended to various reinforcement-learning improvement. However, the actor and
critic alternate updates and depend on each other, ultimately leading to unstable training.

                                               22
BPOF [10] employs an alternating iterative optimization framework that jointly adjusts ZF,
SDR and switch-weight parameters, uses a greedy method in the final stage to convert relaxed

weights into actual on/off decisions to reduce search cost, but remains a heuristic approxi-
mation to a nonconvex problem and incurs high measurement and synchronization overhead
in practice. In Teacher-Student learning [21], teacher provides advice to student at criti-

cal states to reduce performance disparity and achieve tail-end performance improvement.
Instructor guidance can improve empirical performance and stability while preserving the stu-
dent’s autonomous exploration ability, yet relies on heuristic domain rules whose inaccuracies

may mislead learning and whose simplified design may limit the ultimate performance ceil-
ing. This thesis’s CDRL adopts a strategy in which the catfish agent randomly perturbs the
main agent during training to encourage the main agent to explore unvisited action spaces

and avoid local optima. In other words, CDRL’s strategy may cause larger fluctuations in the
reward curve during training, but under its high-risk, high-reward characteristic it has greater
chance to achieve better energy-efficiency performance. Similar to BPOF [10], it presents

higher implementation, computational and memory-cost issues.




