    Critic Network: The critic network takes the current state and action as input and

      outputs Q-value, denoted as Q(st , at ), where Φ represents the parameters of the value
      network.

    Actor Network: The actor network receives the current state of the environment as input

                                                      28
      and, based on a policy function, determines the action to take. The policy is represented
      as π θ (st ), where θ denotes the parameters of the policy network, which guides the agent’s

      action selection, as illustrated in Fig. 11.


   Since the channel matrices and component state matrices are complex-valued while neural

networks can only process real numbers, both inputs and outputs are split into real and
imaginary parts. To enhance training stability, batch normalization is introduced in the hidden
layers, normalizing the outputs of the previous layer to zero mean and unit variance.

   The critic evaluates the value of the actions taken by the actor and provides correction
signals. This is typically measured using temporal difference (TD) error δ, defined as:



                            δ t = rt + γQΦ (st+1 , π θ (st+1 )) − QΦ (st , at )               (4.4)

   Here, π θ (st+1 ) denotes the action given by the actor at the next state. γ is the discount
factor that determines the importance of the future Q-value QΦ (st+1 , π θ (st+1 )), typically rang-
ing between 0.9 and 0.99. Equation 4.4 measures the discrepancy between the current Q-value

estimate and the one-step actual reward plus the estimated Q-value at the next step (target
Q-value). This quantifies the difference between the estimate and reality, which is used to

guide the update of the weighting parameters.
   The critic uses a neural network to approximate the Q-value function Q(st , at ), and updates
the network parameters by minimizing the mean squared error (MSE) loss, defined as:


                                             L(Φ) = (δ t )2                                   (4.5)

   Then, gradient descent is used to update the critic’s parameters Φ as follows:


                                      Φt+1 = Φt − µc ∇Φ L(Φt )                                (4.6)

   Here, µc denotes the learning rate, and ∇ represents the gradient with respect to Φ of the

loss function L(Φt ).



