   Observing equation (4.4), the discount factor causes the weight of the Q-value to decay

exponentially as QΦ (st+1 , π θ (st+1 )) unfolds into the future. The total return is given by rtacc =
rt + γrt+1 + γ 2 rt+2 + γ 3 rt+3 + · · · .Thus, the closer the discount factor is to 1, the slower the
decay, and the greater the emphasis on future rewards, as shown in Fig. 4.8(a) and Fig. 4.8(b).

As shown in Fig. 4.8(a), the discount factor for the main agent is set to γ M , while that for the
catfish agent is set to γ CF . According to Hou et al. [23], when an RL agent approaches the
final outcome, a larger discount factor is beneficial for achieving better results. Accordingly,

based on the prior experience stratification, superior experiences are assigned a larger discount
factor for the catfish agent’s tasks, while ordinary experiences receive a smaller discount factor
for the main agent’s tasks. Therefore, we set γ M ≤ γ CF , allowing the catfish agent to learn

more farsighted and superior policies, while the main agent is relatively myopic compared to
the catfish agent.

C. Catfish Strategy 3: Adaptive Competitive Reward Mechanism

To further enhance the catfish agent’s own capabilities in order to fulfill its role as an elite-
level challenger. We modified the catfish agent’s reward mechanism by incorporating an
adaptive competitive reward mechanism (ACRM). By leveraging advanced heuristic methods

                                                   35
                                Competition




                               Reward
                                                    Competition
                                                    reward
                               Policy Catfish

             Figure 4.9: Illustration of adaptive competitive reward mechanism.


to guide the agent in extracting meaningful value from learning experiences, the stability of
DRL convergence can be improved. The ACRM is similar to the Self-Adaptive Success Rate
Sampling (SASR) proposed by Shen et al. [24]. This thesis expresses the ACRM’s augmented

reward function as:


                                         rC = r + ηrS                                    (4.8)

   Here, r denotes the original EE reward from the environment, rS represents the addi-

tional shaped reward, and η is the coefficient controlling the weight of this reward. When η
approaches 0, the model degrades to the original reward model. As shown in Fig. 4.9, The
additional shaped reward rS at time t is expressed as:



                                        rtS = rtCF − rtM                                 (4.9)

   Here, rtM denotes the reward (energy efficiency) achieved by the main agent in the pas-
sive/active RIS communication system, while rtCF denotes the reward achieved by the catfish

agent in the same system. The greater the lead of the catfish agent over the main agent on
the same task, the larger the additional reward received by the catfish agent. Conversely, the
catfish agent is penalized more when trailing behind, reinforcing the competitive dynamics.

