    Energy efficiency is an overall system performance indicator and is defined as the ratio

     of sum rate to total energy consumption, evaluating the transmission capability per unit
     energy.

    Sum rate is the transmission speed metric, representing the total amount of data received

     by all users per unit time, used to assess the capacity of multi-user channel resources.


   To assess the energy-efficiency performance of the proposed reinforcement learning model,
we benchmark it against three methods, including BPOF. In addition to schemes with ad-

justable element switching, configurations with all RIS elements fully activated are included as
reference benchmarks. Fully activated means that all reflecting elements on the RIS cannot be
switched off. This aims to assess the impact of RIS element switching on the energy efficiency

and sum rate of the communication system. The experiments are further divided into active
and passive RIS to evaluate the effect of active RIS amplifiers on system energy efficiency and
sum rate. The proposed CDRL scheme is represented by a red line with solid diamonds. The


