5.3     Ablation Experiment

    This thesis conducts an ablation experiment to elucidate the contributions of its three

catfish strategies to CDRL. In each experiment, one strategy is removed from the complete
CDRL scheme (red line with solid diamonds). The first ablation removes Catfish Strategy 1:
Experience Stratification (orange line with solid circles) and replaces it with an unsegmented

experience-replay buffer. The second ablation removes Catfish Strategy 2: Asymmetric Dis-
counting Strategy (blue line with solid circles), instead using an identical discount factor
(γ M = γ CF = 0.95). The third ablation removes Catfish Strategy 3: Adaptive Competitive

Reward Mechanism (green line with solid circles), so the catfish agent receives no extra reward.
The fourth one uses only Catfish Strategy 1: Experience Stratification (orange line with solid
diamond markers), where the experience replay memory is replaced with classified experiences.

The fifth one uses only Catfish Strategy 2: Asymmetric Discounting Strategy (blue line with
solid diamond markers), where different discount factors are applied (γ M = 0.95, γ CF = 0.99).
The sixth one uses only Catfish Strategy 3: Adaptive Competitive Reward Mechanism (green

line with solid diamond markers), where the catfish agent applies only the additional reward.
        “Transmission rate vs. transmission power constraint” results for the active RIS ab-
lation are plotted in Fig. 5.7. The unsegmented replay yields a moderate performance drop,

likely because all experiences, regardless of reward magnitude, remain equally probable in
sampling. The identical discount-factor ablation causes a larger degradation, suggesting that
similar objectives for catfish and main agents reduce the main agent’s exploratory stimulus.

Omitting the adaptive competitive reward mechanism has the smallest effect, implying that
the baseline reward density already sufficiently drives CDRL training. Using only the pre-
classified experience replay memory yields a moderate improvement, possibly due to the lack

of support from the other two strategies. Using only different discount factors achieves the


                                              51
      Figure 5.7: The ablation experiment of active RIS in terms of energy efficiency.


greatest improvement, which may result from the effective division of tasks. Using only the
catfish agent without the Adaptive Competitive Reward Mechanism shows a smaller improve-

ment, possibly because the additional reward provides limited benefit.


5.4     The RIS Simulator




                              Figure 5.8: The RIS simulator.



                                             52
    We extended the improved BPOF [10] to develop a simulator for the RIS communication
environment, as shown in Fig. 5.8. By configuring the positions of the BS, RIS, and UE, as

well as adjusting detailed parameters of the relevant components, the simulator can evaluate
the EE performance in this communication scenario. Moreover, additional functions such as
RIS element switching were incorporated to enable energy-efficient joint on/off control of RF

chains and RIS elements.




                                             53
Chapter 6

Conclusion

    This thesis proposes a Catfish-effect-based Deep Reinforcement Learning (CDRL) frame-

work for energy-efficient integrated switching control of RF chains and RIS elements in an
RIS-assisted 6G network. Consider a hybrid beamforming architecture at the base station
that combines a baseband digital precoder with an RF analog beamformer. Each element

on the communication devices can be switched independently. Three core designs for the
catfish agent are proposed, all aimed at ensuring its policy performance surpasses the main
agent. First, in the data pre-generation phase, all experiences are routed according to an EE

threshold. Second, the catfish agent’s discount factor is set larger to focus on long-term return
estimation. Third, an adaptive competitive reward mechanism is implemented for the catfish
agent, encouraging it to continually maintain its performance lead. Through the dual-agent

design enables the Catfish Agent to guide the Main Agent toward better policies and avoid
local optima. Simulation results show that CDRL outperforms baseline DRL, analytical meth-
ods, and teacher-student models under both active and passive RIS settings. Future work may

explore extending the framework to more practical or large-scale scenarios.




