# Reading evidence: terrestrial association/coordination and channel context

Scope: reading only. Read Ye (2013) §§I–VII and Appendix A, Tervo (2017) §§I–VIII (equations/methods and every numerical-results paragraph), and skimmed/read relevant model and results sections of Yu–Kim (2022) and Kim et al. (file dated 2023, actually GLOBECOM 2021 in PDF). Inspected rendered figures Ye Fig. 6 and Tervo Figs. 1–9/Table I, especially figures omitted from extracted text. No experiment or project modification.

## 1. Ye et al., *User Association for Load Balancing in Heterogeneous Cellular Networks*, IEEE TWC 12(6), June 2013, pp. 2706–2716

### Essential interpretation

- Objective is sum logarithmic user rates, **not energy efficiency**. The model fixes BS transmit powers during association and assumes full buffers (§II, pp.2707–2708): “transmit power of BSs is fixed over the association time scale and thus is independent of the specific association.” Fast fading is averaged; low-mobility applicability is stated. The users per BS determine load under full buffers. Equal resource allocation is optimal for its fixed-association logarithmic-utility model (§III-D, Proposition 1, p.2709): “The optimal resource allocation is equal allocation”. This is a useful multiplexing parallel to the project, but not its power-to-rate-target/discrete-MCS objective.
- Its term **joint association** means allowing an individual user to be served by multiple BSs, not moving multiple users simultaneously. §III-B (p.2708), Assumption 1: “We assume that users can be associated with more than one BS at the same time.” §III-D uses this as an upper bound, then focuses on single-BS association; §III-E separately relaxes association indicators to fractional values and rounds.
- Distributed dual method is an iterative coordinated-price algorithm, not a certified one-user-at-a-time best-response fixed point: each user selects argmax(log rate minus broadcast price), BSs update prices; all users can update in an iteration (§IV-B, p.2711, Eqs.15–17). Thus this paper does not directly measure the project's simultaneous-multiuser search premium over unilateral fixed points.

### All relevant comparisons/gaps

1. **Joint (multi-BS) upper bound vs fractional rounding vs dual distributed algorithm**. §III-E (p.2710): “there is almost no loss after rounding”; §VI-B (p.2713): “The CDFs of fractional-rounding and the dual distributed algorithm almost overlap”; “The result of joint association is very close to the result of fraction-rounding”. No single scalar relative gap or pooled-EE gap is reported. Fig.6 (p.2714) visually shows approximately 3.8× max-SINR for joint association versus ~3.6× for dual distributed at the 10th-percentile rate (a roughly 5% relative difference at that percentile); near-coincidence by much of the rest of the CDF. These are graph readings, not tabulated percentages. It is legitimate to report “near-overlap, no aggregate percentage given,” not an exact zero.
2. **Load-aware joint/fractional/distributed/simple bias methods vs max-SINR baseline**. Abstract (p.2706) reports “3.5x” cell-edge throughput and “2x” median rate; §VI-B (p.2713) says “showing a 2-3.5x gain, in both static setting and stochastic setting.” Fig.6 caption (p.2714) expressly says all curves are ratios **to max-SINR**. These are user-rate quantile gains, not aggregate energy efficiency and not gains against converged distributed optimization. Fig.6 is not exactly aligned with the rounded prose claim: visually ~3.6–3.8× at the 10th percentile, ~1.4× at the median for optimized curves. Preserve the authors' reported rounded 2–3.5× as textual claims if used; do not present it as a new precise figure reading.
3. **Optimized associations vs simple per-tier SINR/rate bias**. §V (p.2712): “The network-wide performance with either biasing factor gets pretty close to the optimal FUA, among which rate bias performs better than SINR bias.” Fig.6: around 10th-percentile rate, joint ~3.8× baseline, rate bias ~3.15×, SINR bias ~3.05× (hence joint about 20–25% above simple biases at that percentile); disparity shrinks substantially elsewhere. No aggregate percentage. Bias factors themselves were selected using expensive optimal results: best SINR bias via brute force and rate bias from optimal dual multipliers (§V). Do not imply arbitrary untuned geometry-only association is already optimal.
4. **Macro-only networks**. §VI-B/Fig.5 (p.2713) show much smaller benefit over max-SINR; authors explicitly restrict their conclusion: “the rate gain is unique for HetNets as long as the users are uniformly distributed.” No precise percentage is provided. This is evidence that *load asymmetry* creates large baseline gains, not that simultaneity itself is valuable.

### Conditions and limits

- §I-A (p.2706): “massive disparities in cell sizes” cause unequal max-SINR loads in HetNets; macro-only coverage/load disparity is constrained under uniform users. §VI-A (p.2713): max-SINR overloads macro BSs while some small BSs are idle; shifting traffic yields the gain. Simulation: three tiers, transmit powers 46/35/20 dBm, small-cell densities 5 and 20 per macrocell (§VI, p.2712). Large load-aware-vs-geometry gains do not establish a large joint-vs-good-iterative gain.
- §VI-C (p.2714), Figs.7–8: best bias factors weakly depend on BS density in the studied ranges and strongly on tier transmit powers. This is a statement about optimal bias parameter choice, not evidence that a binding power cap enlarges a coordinated-search premium.
- No experiment sweeps users per beam (not satellite beams), power constraints, or interference coupling to isolate the incremental gain over a converged unilateral method. In fact interference does not depend on association under the model (§II). Cannot transfer this into a general claim about the project’s energy metric.

### Q3 budget/runtime

- §IV-B (p.2711) reports O(N_B N_U) complexity per iteration and fewer than 20 iterations in simulation; the full sentence provides the direct decision-interval criterion: **“It is applicable as long as the convergence of distributed algorithm is faster than the association period.”**
- No wall-clock per-decision runtime, hardware runtime table, numerical association interval, or common wall-clock budget comparison. Its upper bound/rounded/distributed rate results are endpoint/converged comparisons, not a time-capped tournament.
- §IV (p.2710) motivates distributed computation using slow centralized adaptation, coordination complexity and reliability; §IV-B calls gradient convergence fast. This recognizes speed/practical timeliness as relevant, without validating any specific 30.08-second protocol.

## 2. Tervo et al., *Energy-Efficient Beam Coordination Strategies With Rate-Dependent Processing Power*, IEEE TSP 65(22), Nov.15 2017, pp.6097–6112 (accepted/published July 2017)

### Essential interpretation

- This optimizes **beamforming vectors/powers** for fixed user-to-BS serving sets, not satellite handover/user association. §II-A (p.6099): “Each user ... is served only by a single BS”; §II-C/Eq.10 (pp.6100–6101) defines pooled network EE as sum rates over total power, Eq.11 separately defines weighted sum of individual BS EEs. The latter must not be substituted for pooled EE.
- Centralized algorithms use successive convex approximation (SCA); distributed implementations use KKT-based closed-form beamformer updates plus alternating receiver updates (§§III–IV). §I (p.6098) explicitly calls SCA a “local optimization method.” These are not global optimality certificates or unilateral association fixed points.
- §VII (p.6106) uses seven-cell wraparound; each user is interfered by six neighboring BSs, inter-BS distance 120m, cell radius60m, “the cell edges are overlapping and the users are randomly dropped to the cell edges”. L=users/cell, N=antennas/BS; |K|>N throughout (§V, p.6105). These stress interference and degrees-of-freedom scarcity. Table I: per-BS max P_b=27dBm; W=20MHz; coherence block U=100 channel uses; Q=20; efficiency L_BS=12.8Gflops/W. No empirical CPU runtime.

### All comparisons relevant to joint/coordinated vs iterative/greedy

1. **Proposed SCA NetEE algorithm vs DB-WMMSE (Dinkelbach plus WMMSE) iterative method that ignores rate-dependent power during optimization**. §VII Fig.3 (p.6107): gains “up to 60% ... in the considered setting.” At m=1, **exactly no optimizer change/no gain** is predicted (§III-A Remark2 p.6101 and §VII p.6107) because a common linear rate-power term becomes a constant after reciprocation. For m>1, gain grows with rate-dependent processing cost; paper says “Larger gains can be achieved in the systems with low transmit power” where processing power matters (§VII p.6107). Fair evaluation includes actual rate-dependent cost for both after optimization. This is a better power-objective model versus a mismatched iterative optimizer, **not** evidence that simultaneous assignments beat unilateral convergence by60%.
2. **Proposed WsumEE SCA algorithm vs existing parametric iterative WsumEE method [19] ignoring rate-dependent processing in optimization**. Fig.4 (§VII p.6108). Text says a gain exists even for linear m=1 (§VII p.6107), but reports no exact scalar percentage. Figure (visually) m=1 gives small gap, ~89 versus ~87 Mbit/J at largest P_RD≈8.17; nonlinear m=1.2/1.3 gives larger gaps. Avoid promoting this weighted-sum metric to pooled EE. Both are iterative coordinated beamformers; objective mismatch is the intervention.
3. **Proposed coordinated KKT NetEE and Alg.3 beamformers vs (a) multicell MMSE, (b) single-cell MMSE, (c) orthogonal access, (d) uncoordinated full-band access**. Fig.5/§VII (p.6108). No tabulated percentage. Visual estimates across N=3–11, L=3: proposed pooled NetEE≈10.2→15.0 Mbit/J, MC-MMSE≈8.3→11.7, SC-MMSE≈5.5→9.9, orthogonal≈7.5→7.1 (peaks ~8), uncoordinated≈2.8→4.8. Indicative relative-gain ranges are ~20–45% over MC-MMSE, ~50–95% over SC-MMSE, ~35–110% over orthogonal, ~200–265% over uncoordinated. All are approximate readings, not authors' stated percentage claims. The NetEE-KKT and Alg.3 (whose design objective is WsumEE) almost coincide in pooled EE.
   - Crucial comparator definition: MMSE **directions are fixed**, then powers are **iteratively optimized** for network EE (multicell) or BS-specific EE (single-cell). Proposed algorithms optimize directions too. Uncoordinated and orthogonal methods optimize each BS's own EE; full-band uncoordinated case causes “severe inter-cell interference”. Orthogonal case gives each BS W/7 bandwidth. These are differences in cooperation and resource/power design, not searches over the same association neighborhood.
   - Computational accounting deliberately favors MMSE: §VII p.6108 assumes Q=1 for MMSE power cost despite its power optimization being iterative, versus Q=20 for proposed algorithms, so “MMSE consumes 1/20 fraction of the computational power”. It is not a common runtime cap.
4. **Distributed Alg.3/low-OTA-overhead variants versus centralized alternating receiver/beamformer optimization**. Fig.8/§VII (p.6109): **“all the variants of Alg. 3 converge to the centralized solution”**. Two channel realizations end around79 and76 Mbit/J WsumEE, visual coincidence. Low-overhead variants do s=4 or8 BS-side beamformer updates per OTA receiver update and approach the same endpoint in fewer OTA iterations. No reported endpoint percentage advantage. §IV p.6105 caveat: combining loops forfeits formal convergence guarantee, but empirical convergence to centralized solution occurs for proper step size (ρ=.15 in simulations).
5. **Energy-aware grouped pilot allocation Algorithm4 + Algorithm1 versus conventional greedy path-gain pilot assignment + Algorithm1**. §VI Algorithm4 (p.6106) is itself a heuristic/greedy grouping method, not a global assignment solver. Comparator changes κ group ranking from estimated EE (including interference/circuit cost) to sum path gains. Fig.9/§VII (p.6109), N=5,L=3,total21 users: proposed peak at15 UL pilots ~15.7 Mbit/J versus greedy~14.0 (approximately12% pooled-EE gain); around16 pilots ~15.5vs13.9 (~12%); at20–21 resources curves coincide near15 Mbit/J. At the most constrained endpoint11 resources both are near7, so do not generalize to a monotonic ever-growing scarcity gain. Text says with many pilots greedy is “only slightly worse”; when fewer are available it is “significantly inferior”. The result concerns reducing pilot contamination while saving data resources, **not** simultaneous association moves. Their15-pilot setting spends36% of block on DL+UL pilots versus42% for fully orthogonal21-pilot allocation.

### When gain becomes large—what is and is not evidenced

- Interference/overlap/edge users and insufficient spatial degrees of freedom: §§I,V,VII (pp.6098,6105–6106,6108–6109), Fig.5, provide a demonstrated large coordinated-vs-uncoordinated beamforming gap. Authors explain joint direction weights allow stronger control of interference than fixed equal-weight MMSE (§VII p.6109). But they do not sweep overlap or interference strength independently, so these are the setting and mechanism, not a measured law that every increase in overlap enlarges a joint-assignment gap.
- Scarce pilot resources: Fig.9 directly varies available resources and compares interference-aware versus path-gain greedy allocation; a middle scarcity range gives ~12%, ample resources closes it. §VII says grouping spatially separated users can reuse pilots with low mutual interference. This is relevant coordination pressure, distinct from beam-user multiplexing.
- Low transmit power creates a larger benefit from **correctly modeling processing power** (§VII p.6107, Fig.3), not specifically a larger gain from joint versus unilateral assignments. No sweep of a binding cap isolates the latter. L fixed in main beamforming/pilot comparisons; N varies. No user-per-beam threshold claim can be extracted.
- The clean optimized-method endpoint comparison gives convergence to the same centralized solution (Fig.8), not a large central premium. The large gaps in Fig.5 rely on restricted directions, local objectives or omitted interference coordination.

### Q3 computation/decision budget evidence

- No wall-clock seconds per optimization/decision and no shared wall-clock deadline across named algorithms. This paper measures **iteration count, OTA signaling, and modeled computing energy** instead.
- §II-A p.6099 coherence time T_C and coherence block U=W_C T_C provide a physical time scale; Table I U=100 and W=20MHz. Its100μs example in footnote2 is illustrative code-block practicality, not a measured decision runtime or the experiment's deadline. Do not quote it as one.
- §IV Remark3 p.6105 explicitly says sequential transmitter/receiver updates need more pilot resources, reducing data time, and: **“we have to define the maximum number of over-the-air iterations which leaves sufficient amount of resources for data transmission.”**
- **Actual iteration-budget experiment:** §VII p.6109/Fig.6 p.6108: “we set maximum number of iterations Q and stop the algorithm after Q iterations”. Each iteration's computational power is included via Eq.39; for N=7,L=3 best WsumEE at~15–20 iterations; after that EE falls as computing cost dominates beamformer improvement. Fig.6 has one algorithm varying Q, **not competing algorithms under a common wall-clock budget**. Q=1 yields about84 Mbit/J; maximum about95–96; Q=50 about88 atN7 (figure approximations).
- **Faster convergence recognized:** Fig.8 directly presents low-overhead variants as achieving the centralized solution with fewer OTA iterations. Quotes §IV p.6105: “to improve the convergence speed” and §VII p.6109: “OTA signaling overhead can be reduced significantly”. This supports speed/overhead as an accepted contribution in this paper's field. It does not guarantee referee acceptance of a specific project protocol.
- §VII p.6106 stopping tolerance for other figures: last-five-iteration change<10^-4; Figs.1–2 show near-stabilization around10 iterations, strict stopping can take longer. Initial points can speed convergence. Need distinguish training convergence, iteration endpoints, and actual wall-clock decisions.

## 3. Yu & Kim, *Analysis of SINR according to Elevation Angle in Earth Fixed Beam*, APCC2022, pp.633–634

Use: physical coupling context only; no optimization-method comparison, no budgeted algorithm evaluation.

- §II p.633: one600km LEO,61 beams;190 users in19 central beams (10/beam); full frequency reuse. §III p.634: simulation horizon3000s, not algorithm runtime; elevation10°–90°. At2GHz SINR~−17.5dB at10° versus~−1.2dB at90°.
- §III p.634 quote: “As the elevation angle increases, the influence of neighboring beam interference decreases.” Its model links geometry/elevation to interference, but does not show what coordination gain that yields.
- §I p.633 mentions simultaneous handovers can cause congestion, but supplies no joint-versus-unilateral test or magnitude. Cannot turn this introductory remark into evidence for the project's component claim.

## 4. Kim et al., *Downlink Analysis of LEO Multi-Beam Satellite Communication in Shadowed Rician Channels*

Filename says2023_09 but PDF conference banner/copyright/DOI says **GLOBECOM2021**, DOI10.1109/GLOBECOM46510.2021.9685293. Cite identity rather than silently treating filename date as publication date.

Use: physical coupling context and its limits only; no assignment optimizer, greedy comparison, decision runtime or common budget.

- §§II–III PDFpp.2–3: one satellite, spot beams share propagation channel to a given user, frequency reuse1; SIR independent of common shadowing because desired and interfering paths co-vary. Rate or power alone is not a coordination experiment.
- §V PDFp.5 simulation:500km LEO,19 beams,2GHz,30MHz,12.6km cells,30dBi antennas,4dBW/MHz per transmitter; varied elevation and shadowing.
- §V PDFp.6, Figs.4–5: **“the main lobes of the spot beams begin to overlap, driving up inter-cell interference”** as elevation decreases from90°. Direct support for overlap→more interference under this model; no coordination-gain numbers.
- Counterweight against blanket interference claims, same section: **“With heavy shadowing ... the system becomes fully noise-limited”**; Fig.4 no-shadowing almost all users interference-limited, heavy shadowing INR almost exclusively<0dB and substantial below−10dB. Paper's introduction notes low-interference settings include wider cells/less aggressive reuse (§III-A PDFp.3). Therefore geometry/overlap does not ensure coordination headroom if channel/power regime suppresses interference.

## Safe synthesis for root

- These papers do **not** establish a general law that joint assignments outperform a certified unilateral fixed point by large percentages. Ye strongly illustrates that a giant gain over max-SINR can coexist with an almost identical optimized iterative endpoint; Tervo Fig.8 gives equal centralized/distributed endpoints. They concern different objective functions/optimization variables from the user's exact setting. Say corpus is consistent with little residual gain after a good iterative procedure, but does not prove this generally or quantify the project's unbounded global gap.
- Strong coordination gains here arise versus omission of load balancing, restrictions on beamformer directions/local objectives, substantial inter-cell interference, or a greedy pilot score ignoring interference. Those causal/comparator distinctions must accompany every quoted large percentage.
- Timeliness has explicit precedent (Ye association-period requirement; Tervo finite OTA iteration cap and compute-energy tradeoff). **No common wall-clock contest in these assigned sources.** These support legitimacy of studying deployable deadline performance, not a referee guarantee or blanket license to weaken a comparator. The pre-result contract question is a methodological judgment from project facts, not answered empirically by these PDFs.

