# Reading notes: REDA, DLBIA-BH, Resource Allocation (diagnostic only)

Only document reading and PDF rendering were performed. All locations below are printed page numbers, with PDF page numbers where useful. The two required papers were read throughout their substantive body; references were skimmed. The extra Resource Allocation paper was skimmed throughout and read closely in §§II-B, III, IV, V, with Tables I and Figs. 3–4 visually checked. Supplied PDF text contains no arXiv supplement for REDA.

## 1. Holder, Jaques, Mesbahi: Multi Agent Reinforcement Learning for Sequential Satellite Assignment Problems (AAAI-25; REDA)

### What is and is not joint-versus-unilateral here

The immediate assignment solver α is already a joint, globally optimal assignment under its one-agent/one-task constraints. Technical Preliminaries, p26517, Eq. (1), assigns each agent exactly one task and each task at most one agent. It calls this a problem whose solution “can be easily obtained in polynomial time.” The greedy comparator is α applied to CURRENT benefits, not one-at-a-time association improvements. Method / “Bootstrapping from a greedy policy,” p26520: “make the greedy assignment with respect to the current benefit matrix at every time step, without regard for future benefits.” REDA applies the same α mechanism to learned estimates of long-term benefit. Introduction p26517: contribution is to integrate existing greedy planners into MARL and improve “their solution for better long-term planning.”

HAAL is also not the project's certified unilateral fixed point: Related Work / Classical methods, p26518, says HAAL “uses information from several time steps to generate assignments in the style of model-predictive control,” limited to deterministic SAPs. IQL and IPPO learn independent individual actions; they are not iterative optimization of the same pooled objective to a unilateral fixed point. Method p26520 allows decentralized bidding “until they are matched with the task for which they are willing to pay more than all other agents”; this itself is an iterative coordination mechanism.

### Every empirical comparator in this paper

* Toy dictator environment: three agents, three tasks, ten time steps, one agent's choice determines next state. Empirical Experiments / “Does REDA encourage unselfish behavior?”, pp26520–26521, Fig. 2. REDA achieves exact total reward60. Greedy α(current benefits), IQL, IPPO converge near37.8 (≈38 by plot), so REDA gains ≈59%; COMA55 by plot, gain9.1%. The text explicitly says IQL/IPPO “reliably converge to the greedy joint policy” (2,3,1); COMA converges (1,3,1). The toy's greedy reward37.8 follows the given matrices (initial9 plus nine steps3.2), consistent with Fig.2. This is convergence of learned policies, not certification of same-objective unilateral optimization. The cause is a future-state externality: the dictator takes personally valuable task2, creating a low-benefit future state for others. Duplicate-task outcomes are allowed for independent learners with proportionally divided reward.
* Satellite environment: 324 satellites,450 tasks,100-step episodes, task priority+distance benefit, switching penalty, finite battery. Empirical Experiments / “Can REDA be applied to large problems?”, pp26521–26522, Figs. 3–4. Fig.3 at500k training steps: REDA ≈11.5k return; IQL≈9.9k (≈16% gain); IPPO≈7.9k (≈46%); greedy joint α(current benefits)≈2k (≈475%,5.75×); HAAL≈1.1k (roughly+950%,10×). These are deliberately approximate readings of curves, not tabulated results. Introduction p26517 advertises “20 − 50%” over existing approaches across MARL and classical methods, which does not numerically summarize the very large plotted HAAL/greedy gap; do not silently replace the plot with that headline range. Fig.3 reports5 runs with standard deviation shading, exploration decays over300k steps. There is no converged unilateral baseline.
* COMA omitted in satellite environment because its centralized critic over joint assignments is impractical; all other methods receive same reduced observations/actions (top10 tasks, neighbor information,11 actions). IQL additionally receives greedy pretraining; behavior cloning did not benefit IPPO, so it was trained from scratch. Same problem/action constraints here do not mean same learning policy or temporal model.

### Conditions explaining the gap

Satellite batteries start1, spend0.2 per visible-task service, recover0.1 while not serving; once exhausted they earn no reward for rest of episode (p26521). Fig.4 discussion p26522: “Classical approaches like HAAL make consistent assignments, but fail to manage their power state over the course of the episode, running out of power far before the end of the episode.” Independent MARL avoids exhaustion but has assignment conflicts. REDA avoids both. Thus large gains here concern lookahead, battery management, and conflict-free task assignment. This paper does NOT establish gaps as a function of users per beam, load/interference/cell overlap or transmit-power ceilings in a per-rate power controller. Limitations p26522 explicitly says additive separability fails “when satellite beams can cause frequency interference with one another”; REDA as presented assumes only one task per agent and one agent per task. Battery limitations are not equivalent to RF power ceilings.

### Timing

No measured per-decision seconds/milliseconds, no physical duration of a decision step, no common wall-clock budget comparison in supplied PDF. Plots are training steps/test return. p26522 refers reader to supplemental materials on arXiv for “hyperparameter selection, network architecture, and compute requirements”; those materials are not contained in supplied9-page proceedings PDF. This is absence within supplied source, not a claim about the external supplement. Scalability to324 agents and450 tasks is explicit; faster online response is not empirically benchmarked here.

## 2. Li et al.: A Distributed Beam Hopping Strategy With Load Balancing and Coordinated Interference Avoidance for Heterogeneous Satellite Systems (DLBIA-BH; DOI10.1109/TAES.2025.3633205)

### Components and comparators

§IV pp1713–1716: Algorithm1 RW-LB chooses serving satellite probabilistically using available capacity; Algorithm2 BHPO-GA creates local candidate beam-hopping patterns with RT packet survival and intrasatellite interference in fitness; Algorithm3 MC-BHS coordinates patterns across LEO satellites under each MEO. MC-BHS itself is PRIORITY-ORDERED SEQUENTIAL GREEDY selection: §IV-C p1715, “sequentially processes each LEO satellite i based on priority,” selecting the best noninterfering available pattern. It does not coordinate across MEO boundaries (expressly leaves interference there as a computation/communication compromise). This is not exhaustive simultaneous user-association search.

§V p1717 exact baselines:

* Algo1+2: no intersatellite interference handling (isolates addition of MC-BHS).
* Algo2+3: distance-based serving satellite selection, no load balancing (isolates replacing distance with RW-LB).
* LBRIA: Algorithms1+3 with RANDOM beam-hopping.
* LBPIA: Algorithms1+3 with PERIODIC round-robin beam-hopping by cell index.
* LBGIA: Algorithms1+3 with GREEDY beam-hopping, “prioritizing the allocation ... to ground cells with the highest traffic demand.” All three already retain load balancing and intersatellite coordination. Their deficits concern local pattern design (survival deadlines and intrasatellite interference), not lack of all coordination. None is a certified fixed point of the same network objective; GA has a maximum-generation count.

### Comparisons, metrics, and magnitude

Setup (§V p1716, TableII):400 ground cells,4 beams per LEO,5–25 LEOs,8 MEOs,150MHz,40dBW total satellite transmit power; traffic hotspot+Poisson model (§V p1717); averages100 simulations. Headline P0 objective is total realized RT throughput, not pooled EE (§III-B p1712).

1. RW-LB vs nearest satellite (§V pp1716–1717, Fig.5, TableIII). Max-minus-min load utilization disparity for5/10/15/20/25 satellites is RW-LB0.01/0.09/0.11/0.09/0.09 versus nearest0.41/0.77/0.72/0.55/0.47. At15 satellites this is84.7% smaller disparity (0.11 vs0.72). This is not throughput/EE and not simultaneous-user advantage over a load-aware fixed point.
2. Demand sweep160–640Mbps at15 satellites (§V.1 pp1717–1718, Fig.7). At low demand curves nearly coincide; after≈320Mbps the greedy/random/periodic patterns plateau while full scheme grows. At640Mbps reported full RT throughput304.2Mbps, total520.1Mbps, RT satisfaction96%, overall satisfaction82.5%. For Algo1+2, prose gives RT282.3 (another nearby prose range gives281.5: internal small discrepancy), so adding coordination ≈+7.8–8.1%; total comparator upper endpoint472.2 (square curve is upper comparator) gives+10.1%; satisfaction≈92.3% and75.2%, gains3.7pp and7.3pp. For Algo2+3 reported RT235.3, giving+29.3%; total≈403 (visual), ≈+29%; RT satisfaction≈78%, overall≈65% (visual), improvements≈18pp/17.5pp.
3. Same demand sweep compared to three simpler BH baselines. Prose pp1717–1718 supplies their RT range169.6–179.1Mbps at640, full gain≈70–79%; total baseline range339.4–≈365 by plot, full gain≈42–53%. By-eye Fig.7 values for LBGIA specifically: RT≈172Mbps (+≈77%), total≈364Mbps (+≈43%), RT satisfaction≈58% (+≈38pp), overall≈57.5% (+≈25pp). LBRIA visually RT≈179,total≈350, RT satisfaction≈56, overall≈54; LBPIA RT≈170,total≈344, RT satisfaction≈55,overall≈53. Treat all graph estimates as rough; do not imply exact individual values from a range. Paper's prose gives some overlapping/inconsistent endpoints (170.7 vs169.6,281.5 vs282.3).
4. Satellite-count sweep at640Mbps (§V.2 p1718, Fig.8). At25 satellites full RT310.4, total633.2, RT satisfaction99.9%, overall98.7%; all alternatives' ranges RT236.5–309.9,total474–617.2, RT satisfaction76.4–99.8, overall74.1–96.3. The exact per-comparator values below either derive from named prose or are explicitly visual.
5. Addition of interference coordination at25 satellites: named Algo1+2 endpoint309.5Mbps versus310.4, ≈+0.29% RT throughput. Nearby general comparator range says309.9, which would imply+0.16%; source is inconsistent at this precision. Total633.2 vs617.2, ≈+2.6%; RT satisfaction99.9 vs99.8,+0.1pp, overall98.7 vs96.3,+2.4pp (best alternative identified by square line). §V.2 calls performance gap “minimal.” At5 satellites full92.2 vs Algo1+2 84.1,+9.6% RT throughput.
6. Load balancing has NEGATIVE marginal effect at5 satellites: full92.2Mbps vs Algo2+3 105.5Mbps,−12.6%. Explicit §V.2 p1718: “DLBIA-BH’s RT traffic throughput of 92.2 Mbps is slightly lower than Algo 2+3’s 105.5 Mbps.” At25 full310.4 vs Algo2+3 289.3,+7.3%; total/satisfaction plots indicate gains but not exact tabulated per-comparator values.
7. Greedy LBGIA at25 satellites Fig.8: RT≈270 vs310.4,≈+15%; total≈555 vs633.2,≈+14%; RT satisfaction≈87% vs99.9,+≈13pp; overall≈86% vs98.7,+≈13pp. LBRIA reported RT236.5 at25 vs310.4,+31.2%; total474 vs633.2,+33.6%. LBPIA RT≈255,total≈525, gains≈22%/21% (plot estimates). At5 satellites three simpler BH RT≈58–60 vs92.2, about+54–59%; lower-demand and high-resource setting dramatically changes these gaps. Not a local-search convergence study.

### Conditions: strongest evidence and caveats

§V.1 pp1717–1718 shows high demand makes good BH planning more valuable compared with greedy BH, with negligible low-demand throughput gaps and a large high-demand gap. Its stated explanation is that simple methods cannot optimize RT survival time and intrasatellite interference like BHPO-GA. This bundles more than simultaneous assignments.

§V.2 p1718 explains negative load-balancing ablation at5 satellites: “satellites are almost all in an overloaded state,” and distance-based selection “achieves better SINR and thus results in superior throughput.” Spare capacity somewhere is required for useful balancing; uniformly maxed-out satellites can make geometry more valuable. At25 satellites, “satellite resources become abundant, making intersatellite interference less critical to overall performance, thus load balancing becomes the dominant performance factor.” This explicitly supports SMALL marginal coordination gains in one abundant-resource regime; it does NOT say generally small once unilateral search converges. Number of users per beam and power ceiling are not swept independently. Coverage overlap is modeled and interference avoidance matters, but overlap is not swept to identify a quantitative threshold for large gaps.

### Timing: most useful direct precedent

§III-A p1712: one BH frame640ms,64 slots of10ms; resource allocation once/frame. Ground-cell/satellite connections≈10min, treated fixed within frame. They explicitly compute in advance: “During frame i − x, the GA-based BH scheduling computation and control signaling are performed,” where x counts overhead frames.

Footnote1 p1712 reports25 LEOs,400 cells,4 beams,64 slots: GA-based computation613ms measured on Intel Core i9-12900K/Ubuntu22.04; reporting/control≈35ms; combined648ms, “≈ 2 frames.” MEO downlink schedule delivery33ms completes in frame i; LEO executes schedule in frame i+1. This is a concrete computation-and-signaling accounting with lookahead/pipelining, not discarding all solutions taking longer than one640ms interval. Arithmetic648/640≈1.0125; two frames is ceiling, not approximate equality in literal duration.

No per-baseline timing table or common wall-clock-budget optimization comparison. This source gives a reason a referee could question merely declaring65sec unusable in30.08sec: if forecasted inputs are valid far enough ahead, a slower method might pipeline too. Whether that works for project's state-dependent association problem is not answered here and must not be assumed either way.

## 3. Abudureheman et al.: Resource Allocation in Multibeam LEO Satellite Systems Based on Beam Hopping and Frequency Reuse (Jan2026; DOI10.1109/JIOT.2025.3594483)

### Relevant comparisons and limits

§III-A pp173–174 proposes iterative user reassignment from largest-radius cluster to neighbors, updating centers, until no further radius reduction. Algorithm1 says “while not converged.” It is itself one-user-at-a-time refinement. Scheduling activates highest weighted pending-traffic beams; PPO jointly selects subchannels/power. Therefore its comparisons are not clean simultaneous assignment vs converged unilateral allocation.

§IV-B p176 Fig.3 compares refined P-center clustering against K-means/K-means++. Verbatim: “The refined P-center achieves 98.6%–98.9% of K-means++’s throughput while outperforming K-means, with faster convergence, aligning with LEO’s real-time demands.” This accepts ≈1.1–1.4% lower throughput than stronger clustering baseline for speed. HOWEVER visual Fig.3 plots blue Refined P-center slightly ABOVE black K-means++ (labels checked), contradicting the prose's direction. Do not treat that precise penalty as robust independently verified measurement. No numeric faster-convergence measurement is supplied.

§IV-B p176 Fig.4 compares proposed clustering/BH with Max-Dur, Min-Dis and Max-SNR (legend). At1000 users, prose gives66.7–76% fewer required hopping cycles for uniform distribution and70.2–89.7% fewer for nonuniform, retaining>92% final coverage. It does not attribute each endpoint precisely to comparator. Max-Dur described as suffering58% coverage fluctuation in dense regions; Min-Dis an80% increase in hopping cycles in massive connectivity. These are hopping cycles to cover users, not CPU iterations or per-decision runtime, and not pooled EE. The chart is visually complex/stacked; do not invent exact per-baseline percentage endpoint assignment. K-means throughput difference is the only direct iterative-clustering comparison; the remaining comparisons are geometric/duration/SNR single-criterion heuristics.

§IV-B p176 says throughput saturates beyond22 active beams because extra beams split resources without new users and reduced spatial isolation increases interference; it reports3.4% spectral-efficiency decline at fixed transmit power. This is a beam-count/power/interference tradeoff, not a clean coordination-premium sweep. §IV-C pp176–177 gives training reward/SINR improvements (SINR−17.2 to−7.1dB; edge beams36.4% greater SINR improvement than centers), not a competing greedy assignment result. Nonuniform clustered users produce local congestion and greater training variance; it does not establish a converged-unilateral gap.

### Timing and recognized speed contribution

§II-B pp169–170 describes slots Ts milliseconds. Visually checked TableI p175 gives Time step1second, beam hopping period1280second. §III-A p173 claims O(B_t U logU) refinement versus O(U^3) exact P-center formulation and “real-time feasibility,” with no empirical CPU measurements. §III-B p174 states per-slot online worst-case complexityO(I+B_tK+UK); again no latency benchmark or common wall-clock cap. §V p178 explicitly cautions deployment is “constrained by onboard computational capabilities and inference latency, potentially affecting real-time responsiveness.” Speed is clearly a stated legitimate contribution, but this paper provides limited runtime evidence and no equal-budget experiment.

## Cross-paper diagnostic limited to these three

None compares bounded simultaneous multi-user association against iterated same-objective unilateral search certified at a fixed point. Therefore none establishes that the true premium over such a fixed point is large OR generally small. REDA's large greedy gap cannot support that claim because greedy already solves current JOINT assignment and loses on temporal/battery logic. DLBIA-BH has explicit small and negative marginal component effects in some regimes. Its high-demand greedy BH gap is mainly about better pattern design, packet deadlines and interference; its coordination algorithm itself is sequential.

On the user's numbers (treated as stated measurements),0.51% is evidence of a small extra gain from the tested bounded joint search over that certified fixed point in that setting;442% vs nearest is evidence that load-/objective-aware optimization beats geometry-only. Neither number isolates each learned component's contribution. Deadline evaluation is a distinct scientific target: achieved utility by operational deadline can be legitimate, and prior contract specification is useful anti-selection evidence, but these sources do not settle referee acceptance or preregistration sufficiency. Common budget, feasible incumbent/fallback, same hardware/information, all scheduling+signaling costs, and disclosure of converged reference remain relevant as methodological judgment; five seconds is not30.08 seconds. DLBIA-BH additionally makes pipeline/lookahead alternatives material rather than hypothetical. No experiment was run to decide these matters.

