# Evidence notes for Q2/Q3: handover, uplink, and HARQ papers

Reading only. Used the supplied PDFs and extracted layout text; rendered figures solely to read their plotted values. No project code or experiment was run, and no files in `/home/sat/mcrl-leo-handover` or its virtual environment were touched. Values marked ≈ are visual readings, not author-tabulated values. Article-page numbers are used where printed; HOBS uses PDF-page numbers.

## 2026 dual-agent handover/power paper — mandatory, read relevant body in full

**Source:** Qingyuan Zhang, Shu Fu, Zhaohui Yang, *Jointly Optimizing Satellite Handover and Power Allocation in LEO Satellite Network: A Dual-Agent Framework*, DOI 10.1109/TVT.2026.3674597, supplied accepted author manuscript, pp. 1–6.

### Scope and objective

- §II pp. 2–3: a satellite serves at most two users, a user connects to one satellite per slot; satellite coverage areas overlap; each satellite has maximum power Pmax and bandwidth B. §II-C Eq. (8) includes interference from other users. This is not a beam association/equal-airtime/discrete-MCS model.
- §II-C pp. 3, Eq. (12), optimizes average connected-user rate and number of timed-out users, **not pooled energy efficiency**. Quote: “Since these objectives conflict” the reward produces “a suboptimal compromise rather than a multi-objective global optimum.”
- §III p. 3: “The output of the DQN agent serves as the input for the PPO agent,” then power results feed back to DQN. Thus “joint” refers to handover and power decisions solved through iterative coordination. No comparison against a certified unilateral multiuser association fixed point is reported.

### Comparators and magnitudes

- §IV pp. 4–5, Fig. 3: D2AOF uses DQN handover plus PPO power. DQN+DDPG replaces the continuous optimizer; DQN+RP uses random power, PPO+RH random handover. Author reports reward ≈350 after 120 episodes vs DQN+DDPG ≈300 after 170 episodes, DQN+RP ≈250. Source abstract calls this 16% higher reward vs alternative dual-agent, 40% vs single-agent, 13% average-throughput gain and 30% fewer timed-out users, but the abstract does not identify one common operating point/comparator for the latter two. Training episodes are not online optimization passes or common elapsed-time budgets.
- §IV p. 5, actual heuristic assignment comparators: **GW = Greedy + Water-Filling**, which “greedily schedules users with higher urgency (remaining data / remaining allowable time)” then allocates per-satellite water-filling power. **PW = Polling + Water-Filling**, which “scans users in a rotating order and assigns up to two users per satellite,” then water fills. Neither is an iterative global-utility best-response solver to a certified fixed point.
- §IV p. 5, Fig. 4, average rate vs Pmax=60,100,140,180,220 W. Visual readings: D2AOF≈370,385,390,420,440 Mbps; GW≈165,180,205,220,235 Mbps; PW≈320,335,350,360,380 Mbps. Thus endpoints give D2AOF vs GW ≈+124% at 60 W and +87% at 220 W; vs PW ≈+16% at both endpoints. Prefer raw approximate endpoints to unwarranted precision. DQN+DDPG approaches D2AOF (≈420 vs 440 Mbps at 220 W).
- §IV p. 5, Fig. 5, timed-out users at population 20: all ≈0. Population 60: D2AOF≈2, GW≈27, PW≈17. Population 100: D2AOF≈32, GW≈65, PW≈57. Thus at 100 users, ≈51% fewer timeouts than GW and ≈44% fewer than PW, with absolute reductions ≈33 and ≈25. Those are QoS gaps, not energy-efficiency gains.
- **Explicit load mechanism:** §IV p. 5: “GW performs even worse at high load because its greedy rule repeatedly favors a small set of urgent users.” PW cannot prioritize urgent users and ignores channels. This supports a large gap against these particular myopic heuristics under load; it does not establish an irreducible joint-versus-converged-unilateral gap.
- Power-cap sweep demonstrates relative gains for these methods but does not isolate power caps as a general cause of coordinated-association value. Coverage overlap is modeled (§II p. 2), not swept to quantify this gap. The two-users-per-satellite cap is fixed, not a beam-load sweep.

### Explicit training-signal ablation with negative throughput marginal

- §IV p. 5, Figs. 4–5: “D2AOF-NP excludes the penalty term in the reward function and focuses only on average rate. D2AOF-NP achieves the higher average rate than D2AOF.” Adding timeout penalty reduces average throughput but improves timed-out-user count. Source says including it “significantly improves overall service quality.” This is a reward-term/training-signal ablation and a deliberate conflicting-objective tradeoff. It is secondary contextual evidence for Q1, not evidence about what the catfish source itself requires.

### Q3 runtime evidence

- §IV p. 4: “time slot interval ΔT = 1 s”; maximum data-download time Tmax=450 s is a user deadline, not a computation budget.
- §V-C p. 6, Table I: D2AOF training 10,960.36 s vs DQN+DDPG 12,650.23 s, both 300 episodes. Per-episode mean/median/max 36.37/35.14/65.13 s vs 42.16/45.48/72.02 s. These are training episode durations; do not compare 65.13 s to the project's decision convergence time as though equivalent.
- Table I inference per call: D2AOF DQN 0.0011 s, PPO 0.0035 s; comparator DQN 0.0012 s, DDPG 0.004 s. Training per call is separate: 0.0677/0.370 s vs 0.0750/0.456 s.
- §V-C: “D2AOF requires only about 0.004–0.005s per decision,” “well below typical LEO scheduling timescales”; supports “offline training + online inference.” Its experimental slot is explicitly 1 s (§IV).
- §V-C claims “completes training 15% faster”; actual reduction relative to comparator from table is ≈13.4%, while comparator training is ≈15.4% longer. Best to use table times.
- **No common elapsed-time-cap quality comparison**. It reports convergence rewards, training speed, and average inference latency, comparing the two learned dual-agent architectures. No GW/PW runtime is given and no hard worst-case decision guarantee established.

## 2025 joint uplink resources paper — skimmed and read §§II-C, III-A, IV fully where relevant

**Source:** Zhankui Cai et al., *Energy-Efficiency-Based Joint Uplink Resources Allocation for LEO Satellite Beam-Hopping System*, IEEE IoT Journal 12(19), pp. 41248–41265, DOI 10.1109/JIOT.2025.3592727. Title filename July 2025; supplied journal issue 1 October 2025.

### Scope and metric

- §II-C p. 41251, Eqs. (6)–(8): terminal EE=terminal rate/(terminal transmit+circuit power); objective sums EEs weighted by energy-efficiency sensitivity (EES), across terminals and slots. **Weighted sum EE is not the project's pooled network ratio**.
- §II-B pp. 41250–51: same-beam terminals use FDMA, no same-beam interference, neglect intersatellite uplink interference, model interbeam cochannel interference. §IV-A p. 41257: 144 cells, at most nine beams active, four terminals per beam (576 total). These counts are fixed in baseline setting.

### The directly relevant local-search comparison

- §III-A pp. 41252–54, Algorithms 1–2: BBHMA-1 initializes matching using an improved Gale–Shapley procedure then greedily accepts pairwise swaps of **beams between hopping patterns**, reaching two-sided exchange stability (2ES). Quote p. 41253: it “lacks the ability to escape from local optimum due to its greedy strategy.” BBHMA-2 adds simulated annealing to accept some unfavorable swaps and escape. These are pairwise beam-pattern swaps, not one-user unilateral association changes.
- Stability proof p. 41254: BBHMA-1 terminates at a stable 2ES matching. BBHMA-2 has finite annealing/Markov-chain settings; global optimality with adequately many iterations is discussed but finite experimental runs are not certified globally optimal.
- §IV-B p. 41258, Fig. 3: at threshold WSEE≥45,000 Mbit/Joule for one hopping slot, visually **Pr≈0.78 BBHMA-1 vs ≈0.88 BBHMA-2**, or ≈10 percentage points more threshold exceedance. Other methods: RBH≈0.22, periodic BH≈0.40, spatial-isolation 2-D≈0.46, spatial-isolation 3-D≈0.64, DRL-BH≈0.66. These survival probabilities are labeled “C.D.F” by authors, but mathematically are exceedance probabilities; do not call +10 points a +10% EE improvement. Authors say BBHMA methods beat conventional by “at least 15% in terms of the C.D.F,” and DRL-BH gains “only a 2% performance improvement” over SPBH-3-D at this threshold (plotted +2 points).
- §IV-B p. 41258, Fig. 4, total WSEE over a hopping period: “Regarding BBHMA-1, SPBH-3-D, and DRL-BH, their results do not show significant differences.” Reason given: cumulative WSEE “inherently obscures optimization at the time-slot level.” BBHMA-2 curve is slightly better; no tabulated exact total-period gap. This is an explicit small-gap finding for a particular metric, not a general theorem about unilateral convergence.
- **Most useful magnitudes:** §IV-D p. 41260, Fig. 10 compares all hopping patterns followed by the same JPFA power/frequency optimization. Visual plateau values in total WSEE per BH period: BBHMA-2+JPFA≈46.25×10^4, BBHMA-1+JPFA≈45.7×10^4, DRL-BH+JPFA≈45.5×10^4, SPBH-3D+JPFA≈45.4×10^4 Mbit/Joule. Therefore escaping BBHMA-1's greedy swap optimum is ≈**+1.2%**; BBHMA-1 exceeds DRL-BH and SPBH-3D by only ≈0.4% and ≈0.7%. Curves stationary by roughly five JPFA iterations. These are visual estimates, not published exact percentages.
- §IV-D p. 41260 deliberately uses cheaper BBHMA-1 in remaining experiments: “because the complexity of BBHMA-1 is less than BBHMA-2.”

### Other resource comparisons, metric mismatch, conditions

- §IV-A p. 41257 defines fixed frequency allocation FA plus optimized power, MaxGEE (different objective, no EES), MaxCC (capacity objective), interference-free UP-bound. §IV-D Figs. 10–11 compares joint frequency/power optimization with these, **not joint vs unilateral assignment**. In Fig. 11 JPFA≈28.9k, FA≈24.4k, MaxGEE≈19.2k, MaxCC≈15.2k, UP-bound≈29.1k, UP-bound-real≈27.6k Mbit/Joule. All evaluated after iterations/convergence; proposed vs FA≈+18%, but enlarging optimized variables differs from multiuser association coordination.
- §IV-D p. 41261 source reports “improve the WSEE by 34%” vs ignoring EES. Fig. 11 JPFA vs MaxGEE visually instead implies ≈50% gain with baseline denominator, or ≈34% loss normalized to proposed. Do not present the source's 34% as a standard pooled EE marginal.
- §III-A p. 41252: simultaneous neighboring cells interfere “extremely severe[ly]” within one beam diameter, motivating separation; high EES weights influence which beams share hopping slots. §IV-C p. 41259: ignoring EES makes BBHMA-1 resemble spatial-only SPBH-3-D; excessively emphasizing separation beyond 3D can neglect low-EES beams. This supports coupling and heterogeneous objective weights matter, not “more distance/overlap/users always means a larger coordination gap.”
- §IV-E p. 41261, Fig. 13: rate/EE behavior changes as max terminal power increases; “the gaps between different methods are mainly determined by the frequency allocation result.” Above −4 dBW JPFA reaches stationary WSEE, some alternative objectives deteriorate, FA/MaxGEE can also saturate. No sweep of the BBHMA-2-vs-BBHMA-1 gap by power caps or users-per-beam.
- §IV-B Fig. 5 changes composition of terminal classes and thus EES weights; supports method in different class mixtures but not a pure user-load coordination sweep.

### Q3 timing

- §IV-A p. 41257: “each slot t ... is set to 2 ms”; whole BH period 32 ms. These are physical service intervals.
- §III-A p. 41253: increasing annealing search makes optimal results likelier but “time consumption is also greater,” necessitating balance.
- §IV-B p. 41259, Fig. 6: reducing Markov-chain length imax 10,000→1,000 reduces stated complexity to one tenth while losing some performance. Quote: “a relatively small-scale search can find a local solution”; “it is acceptable to sacrifice part of the performance ... to reduce the algorithm complexity.”
- §IV-C p. 41260, Table II reports mean swap operations: 10,10,11,13,14,18 for distances 1D…6D. This is an operation count, not milliseconds.
- §IV-D p. 41260 selects ten JPFA iterations after demonstrating convergence. No measured wall-clock decision time or common fixed computation-time budget across methods is reported. Finite iteration cap and complexity sensitivity are not common elapsed-time budgets.

## 2024 HOBS paper — relevant skim/read for operational latency and resource coordination

**Source:** Sz-Han Chen et al., *Energy-Efficient Joint Handover and Beam Switching Scheme for Multi-LEO Networks*, VTC2024-Spring, DOI 10.1109/VTC2024-SPRING62846.2024.10683088, supplied PDF seven pages.

- §IV PDF pp. 3–5: HOBS uses historical beam indexes/SINR to restrict scanning, handover based on target SINR plus offsets/time-to-trigger, and iterative per-beam dynamic power control. Baseline EBS exhaustively scans all beams and uses full power, while HOBS-F uses the reduced beam search plus full power; HOBS-P adds dynamic power. **No certified unilateral association fixed-point baseline**.
- §V PDF p. 6 Fig. 4: EBS “always scans all beams to guarantee optimal SINR, but induces unaffordable training overhead.” HOBS approaches EBS SINR while lowering beam acquisition overhead. EBS is exhaustive **physical beam sweeping**, not exhaustive combinatorial multiuser assignment.
- §V PDF p. 6, Fig. 5: authors report HOBS-F and HOBS-P “approximately 1.2 times to twice higher throughput compared to EBS.” Plots support roughly ratios 1.2–2 across settings; avoid interpreting “twice higher” as +200%.
- Fig. 6, pooled sum-rate/transmit-power EE, approximate endpoints at Tf=3 s: low user density 2×10^-5 users/km² HOBS-P≈3.0×10^7 vs EBS≈2.2×10^7 bit/J (≈+36%); higher density 5×10^-5 HOBS-P≈4.0×10^7 vs EBS≈3.6×10^7 (+11%). HOBS-F lies very close to EBS in EE despite its higher throughput. These are power/sweeping comparisons, not a pure coordination gap.
- §V PDF pp. 6–7: higher density increases training time from “insufficient beam resources and high interference”; larger frame intervals make historical beam information stale; simultaneous-satellite beam sweeping suffers interference “especially in the ultra-dense LEO scenario.” EE eventually saturates with user density because beam and power resources become insufficient. No isolated association-coordination marginal quantified against those variables.

### Strong operational-cost-aware comparison, not CPU budget

- §II-B PDF pp. 2–3, Eq. (7): acquisition time Cn includes beam beacon time plus user feedback and acknowledgments. Eq. (9) explicitly deducts acquisition time from rate by multiplying by **(1−Cn/Tf)**, where Tf is physical frame duration. Quote: “a longer beam alignment time will reduce the data transmission time.” This is **beam training**, not neural-network training.
- §III PDF p. 3, Eqs. (14e–f) impose per-satellite and overall beam-training latency thresholds. §V PDF p. 6 varies frames 3–7 s; Table II also spans 2–7 s.
- Fig. 5(b) PDF p. 6 plots EBS acquisition≈1.85 s vs HOBS≈0.75–1.5 s depending density/frame. Absolute transmission-rate penalties arise from the same physical frame-duration model, not truncating computation.
- §I PDF p. 2 explicitly presents smaller beam search space/lower latency as a contribution. No measured CPU runtime, common wall-clock computation cap, or convergence-time comparator.

## 2022 IR-HARQ power-allocation paper — screened, excluded from assignment evidence

**Source:** Hongxiu Bian, Rongke Liu, *Reliable and Energy-Efficient LEO Satellite Communication with IR-HARQ via Power Allocation*, Sensors 22, 3035 (2022), DOI 10.3390/s22083035.

- §§1.2, 2–3: single LEO-to-ground-user IR-HARQ link, comparing variable power across retransmission rounds to equal power and a prior approximate-outage variable-power optimizer; no satellite/beam/user assignment algorithm. Thus its large energy gains cannot answer multiuser coordination Q2.
- §3.2 pp. 11–12: genetic algorithm and big-O complexity in evolution iterations, population, HARQ rounds, FFT size. §4.2 pp. 15–17: more HARQ rounds trade EE against computational complexity and transmission delay; Fig. 12 shows iteration convergence. These are not per-decision wall-clock budgets.
- §2.2 p. 7 mentions channel parameters update every 1 s in DVB-S2, RTT under 20 ms and few HARQ rounds supporting quasi-static channel treatment. Those are channel/protocol times, not measured solver runtimes.
- No coordinated-vs-greedy assignment comparison and no common computation-time-budget experiment found. Exclude from quantitative claim ledger, list as screened and used only to rule out HARQ-as-handover conflation.

## Cross-paper verdicts within this subcorpus

1. Large gaps here occur against heuristics omitting urgency/channel/global fairness, resource variables, or acquisition overhead; they do not establish a large residual beyond a certified utility-improving unilateral fixed point.
2. The closest local-optimum escape example, BBHMA-2 vs exchange-stable greedy BBHMA-1, has approximately +1.2% total-period WSEE gain after common JPFA, and the paper proceeds with the cheaper greedy variant. It is not the same move neighborhood or metric as the project.
3. Fast deployment/inference and explicit physical overhead accounting are recognized contributions in these papers. None in this assigned subcorpus compares solution quality under a common elapsed CPU decision budget. Therefore referee acceptability of the project's contract/budget design requires methodological judgment, not a claim that these sources have already validated that exact comparison.

