@@ -0,0 +1,132 @@
+**Q1 — Stricter bar:** the catfish source shows positive ablation effects, including a very small one, but never requires every mechanism to exceed a prescribed EE margin.
+
+**Q2 — No large residual gap established:** this corpus shows large gains over myopic or poorly coordinated baselines, and several small gaps between competent optimizers; it does not establish a large gain over a certified unilateral association fixed point.
+
+**Q3 — Legitimate with conditions:** decision-time performance is defensible; prior specification rebuts inventing the comparison after results, but alone does not justify promoting it to primary or establish fairness.
+
+**LIT-CLAIM-STRUCTURE-2026-09-10 — `DIAGNOSTIC_NOT_CLAIM`**
+
+This is a literature verdict, not an experimental result or authorization to change the project’s acceptance contract. The +0.51%, +442%, 30.08-second interval, 65-second mean convergence time, and five-second result are supplied project facts, not measurements made here. No project code or experiments were run. The handover repository and its virtual environment were not modified.
+
+All twelve PDFs were inspected. The five required sources were read throughout their relevant definitions, algorithms, evaluations, and limitations; the remaining seven were screened and their relevant sections read more closely. Source identifiers below refer to the supplied PDFs, with printed page numbers unless stated otherwise. Quotation marks identify source wording. Values marked **≈** are visual readings of figures, and percentages calculated from them are approximate, not author-reported estimates or significance tests. “Increase” uses the comparator as denominator; percentage points describe differences between percentages. Findings about referees and the project are explicitly methodological judgments, not claims that the corpus studies peer review.
+
+**Q1: What the catfish source establishes**
+
+The supplied CDRL document is Bo-Han Ke’s July 2025 master’s thesis, not a paper establishing a general acceptance standard. Its abstract says the framework “comprises two agents: the catfish agent and the main agent.” Its three mechanisms belong to that two-agent design; they are not three separately deployed catfish agents. The motivation is an introduced competitor that stimulates exploration: the catfish agent collects “rare, high-reward experiences,” and periodically supplies them to the main agent. The transfer is “experience-level knowledge transfer.” At intervention, the main training batch contains 70% main replay and 30% catfish replay. At deployment, the main actor produces actions and the catfish actor monitors performance. (C, Abstract; §§3.3, 4.2.2, 4.3, pp.21–22, 30–39.)
+
+| Mechanism | Intended role in CDRL | What its leave-one-out ablation actually changes |
+|---|---|---|
+| 1. Experience stratification | Creates an “elite challenger role”: high-EE experiences train the catfish; intermediate-EE experiences train the main agent. The high threshold is the top 20% of generated data; experiences below the top-50% threshold are discarded. | Replaces stratification with “an unsegmented experience-replay buffer.” This changes the training-data distribution, not a physical state input or an inference head. |
+| 2. Asymmetric discounting | Separates short-term and long-term learning objectives to “avoid strategy homogenization.” The main agent emphasizes immediate demands; the catfish emphasizes future returns. | Uses “an identical discount factor,” γM = γCF = 0.95. This changes the learning target/horizon, not the number of agents. The strategy-only arm uses 0.95 and 0.99. |
+| 3. Adaptive competitive reward | Rewards the catfish for outperforming the main agent and penalizes it for trailing: rC = rCF + η(rCF − rM). It supports the challenger’s performance lead. | The catfish “receives no extra reward.” This removes the additional training signal, not the environment reward, an observation input, or the whole catfish agent. |
+
+Sources for the table: C, §4.2.2(A–C), pp.33–37, Eqs.(4.8)–(4.10); §5.3, p.51. These are functional training interventions. The thesis does not document an equal-compute, equal-data-volume placebo protocol for them.
+
+The experiment measures contribution in **two distinct ways**. First, it compares FULL against FULL minus one strategy: “In each experiment, one strategy is removed from the complete CDRL scheme.” Second, it plots three strategy-only arms alongside the basic actor–critic baseline, labelled “AC active RIS.” Thus the leave-one-out reference is **complete CDRL**; the strategy-only reference is **basic AC**, not nearest association, a converged optimization oracle, or hardware with an RF chain physically removed. The main performance section separately compares CDRL with AC, BPOF, and teacher–student learning, and separately contrasts switchable and all-on RIS hardware. Those hardware comparisons are not the three training ablations. (C, §§5.2–5.3, pp.41–42, 51–52, Fig.5.7.)
+
+The ablation’s headline is total EE. The caption and vertical axis of Fig.5.7 say energy efficiency, although §5.3 mistakenly calls the experiment “Transmission rate vs. transmission power constraint.” The text describes a moderate loss without stratification, a larger loss without asymmetric discounting, and the **“smallest effect”** without competitive reward, “implying that the baseline reward density already sufficiently drives CDRL training.” It calls the reward’s benefit “limited.” (C, §5.3, pp.51–52.)
+
+At the 20-dBm endpoint, Fig.5.7 visually gives FULL ≈46.6, without strategy 1 ≈45.7, without strategy 2 ≈45.3, and without strategy 3 ≈46.3 bit/Hz/J: roughly **+2%, +3%, and +0.6%**, respectively, for FULL relative to each ablation. The AC baseline is ≈43.4; strategy-only arms are ≈44.2, 44.6, and 43.6, respectively. The third strategy’s standalone gain is therefore only about **0.5%** at that endpoint. These are approximate illustrations, not tabulated margins. The plot has no uncertainty bars or reported per-component significance test. (C, §5.3, Fig.5.7, p.52.)
+
+**Does C report a zero or negative component contribution? No explicit such result is reported.** Its plotted ordering supports positive effects at the illustrated endpoint, with competitive reward contributing very little. “Smallest effect” and “limited benefit” must not be rewritten as “zero,” “harmful,” or proof of a statistically positive gain everywhere. The text also contains two presentation inconsistencies: its strategy-2/3 color descriptions disagree with the figure legend, and its final strategy-only explanation says “without” competitive reward after defining that arm as using only that reward mechanism. The interpretation above follows the named strategy definitions and figure legend; the thesis does not resolve those inconsistencies. (C, §5.3, pp.51–52.)
+
+**The owner’s requirement is stricter than the source’s demonstrated bar.** The source’s phrase “all aimed at ensuring its policy outperforms that of the main agent” describes the joint design intent of the catfish’s three mechanisms. It does not state that each must independently deliver a specified positive margin in system EE. Its evidence does go in the positive direction in both ablation styles, so it would also be inaccurate to say C provides no individual-effect evidence. What the owner adds is a mandatory quantitative acceptance threshold for every component, transferred to the project’s different system and power model. C itself already uses a summed-rate/total-power ratio. If “individually” instead means three independent deployed controllers or additive gains that must sum, that is also a different architectural/causal claim: neither ablation establishes additivity. (C, §3.2, Eqs.(3.12a), (3.13a); §4.2.2, p.30; §5.3, pp.51–52; Chapter 6, p.54.)
+
+The literature does not cancel that project requirement. Relaxing it would be a project decision that must be stated openly; it cannot be attributed to C. Conversely, a component can legitimately serve a supporting learning role without satisfying the owner’s chosen margin, but that would support a narrower claim than the existing acceptance requirement. This is the methodological consequence of the source’s design and ablation distinctions above.
+
+**Q2: The comparison evidence, with the comparators kept intact**
+
+“Joint,” “distributed,” and “iterative” are not opposites in these sources. Several proposed coordinated algorithms are themselves sequential or iterative. None of the twelve PDFs reports the exact project comparison: simultaneous multi-user association moves versus the same objective optimized by unilateral moves to a certified fixed point. The ledger below covers the relevant comparison families, giving reported magnitudes or representative figure readings rather than inventing a single aggregate percentage where the source provides only curves.
+
+**Y — User association and load balancing (2013).** This is the most useful warning against confusing a weak-baseline gain with a coordination premium. It optimizes sum-log user rates at fixed BS powers; its equal-allocation result is relevant to multiplexing, but it is not the project’s rate-target power controller. Moreover, “joint association” means that users “can be associated with more than one BS at the same time,” not that several users change single-BS associations together. Its distributed algorithm coordinates choices through iterated prices; it is not a unilateral best-response certificate. (Y, §§II, III-B–D, IV-B, pp.2707–2711.)
+
+| Comparison in Y | Magnitude and meaning |
+|---|---|
+| Fractional centralized solution/rounding versus distributed price iteration | “The CDFs … almost overlap”; “there is almost no loss after rounding.” No aggregate percentage is supplied. |
+| Multi-BS joint-association upper bound versus fractional rounding | “Very close.” Fig.6 suggests only a few percent difference at the lower rate quantile and near coincidence elsewhere; no pooled-EE result. |
+| Optimized association versus max-SINR | Authors report “2–3.5x gain,” including an abstract claim of 3.5× cell-edge and 2× median rates. These are rate gains against max-SINR, not against the converged distributed optimizer. |
+| Optimized association versus tuned rate/SINR bias | Bias methods get “pretty close to the optimal FUA”; at the 10th-percentile rate, Fig.6 suggests joint ≈3.8× max-SINR versus ≈3.15× rate bias and ≈3.05× SINR bias: ≈20–25% more at that quantile. Much smaller differences occur elsewhere. |
+| Macro-only network versus heterogeneous network | Macro-only benefit over max-SINR is much smaller; the source says its large rate gain is “unique for HetNets as long as the users are uniformly distributed.” No single percentage is given. |
+
+Sources: Y, §§III-E, V, VI-A–B, pp.2710–2714, Figs.5–6. The abstract’s rounded rate-gain summary is not numerically identical to every Fig.6 quantile; it should remain an attributed summary. The mechanism is unequal loading from “massive disparities in cell sizes”: macro cells overload while small cells have spare capacity. It demonstrates that a very large geometry/SINR-baseline gap can coexist with almost identical optimized endpoints. (Y, §I-A, p.2706; §VI-A–B, p.2713.)
+
+**R — Sequential satellite assignment, REDA (2025).** Its greedy comparator already solves the **joint current-step assignment** optimally. What makes it greedy is acting “without regard for future benefits.” REDA supplies learned long-term values to that assignment mechanism. HAAL uses several future steps in model-predictive fashion; independent IQL/IPPO policies have different coordination failures. None is the project’s certified unilateral optimizer. (R, Technical Preliminaries, Eq.(1), p.26517; Related Work, p.26518; Method, “Bootstrapping from a greedy policy,” p.26520.)
+
+| Environment and comparison | Magnitude |
+|---|---|
+| Three-agent, ten-step dictator example | REDA return 60 versus greedy/IQL/IPPO ≈38: ≈58–59% gain. COMA ≈55: ≈9% gain. |
+| 324 satellites, 450 tasks, 100-step episodes; Fig.3 endpoint | REDA ≈11,500 versus IQL ≈9,900: ≈16%; IPPO ≈7,900: ≈46%; current-step greedy ≈2,000: ≈475%; HAAL ≈1,100: roughly a tenfold return. |
+
+Sources: R, Empirical Experiments, pp.26520–26522, Figs.2–4; figures average five runs. Its introductory “20–50%” summary does not describe every plotted comparator, especially greedy/HAAL. The large classical-method gap occurs because they “fail to manage their power state … running out of power far before the end of the episode.” Independent learners instead produce assignment conflicts. This is evidence for temporal planning, battery management, and conflict avoidance. The limitation section expressly excludes general interference coupling: additive rewards fail “when satellite beams can cause frequency interference with one another.” It also assumes one task per agent and at most one agent per task. (R, Introduction, p.26517; Empirical Experiments and Limitations, p.26522.)
+
+**B — Distributed beam hopping, DLBIA-BH (2025/2026).** This source separates load balancing, local pattern optimization, and intersatellite interference coordination. The coordinator itself “sequentially processes each LEO satellite … based on priority.” Its greedy/random/periodic baselines **already retain load balancing and intersatellite coordination**; they simplify the local beam-hopping pattern. Thus their loss is not the whole value of coordination. (B, §IV-C, p.1715; §V, p.1717.)
+
+| Comparator | Full DLBIA-BH advantage at 640-Mbps demand, 15 satellites | At 25 satellites, same demand |
+|---|---|---|
+| Algo1+2: omit intersatellite coordination | RT throughput 304.2 versus ≈282 Mbps: ≈8%; total 520.1 versus ≈472.2 Mbps: ≈10% | RT 310.4 versus 309.5 Mbps: ≈0.3%; total 633.2 versus 617.2 Mbps: ≈2.6% |
+| Algo2+3: nearest-satellite association, omit load balancing | RT 304.2 versus 235.3 Mbps: ≈29%; total ≈29% | RT 310.4 versus 289.3 Mbps: ≈7.3% |
+| LBGIA: demand-greedy local hopping | RT ≈77%; total ≈43% | RT ≈15%; total ≈14% |
+| LBRIA: random local hopping | RT ≈70%; total ≈49% | RT ≈31%; total ≈34% |
+| LBPIA: periodic local hopping | RT ≈79%; total ≈51% | RT ≈22%; total ≈21% |
+
+Sources: B, §V.1–2, pp.1717–1718, Figs.7–8. Individual random/periodic/greedy entries use approximate curve readings; prose gives ranges rather than a table of every endpoint. The source inconsistently gives ≈281.5/282.3 and 309.5/309.9 Mbps for nearby baseline descriptions, so the tiny high-resource marginal is defensibly **about 0.2–0.3%**, not a precise universal 0.29%. At high demand the simpler pattern methods plateau, while at low demand their throughput nearly coincides. RT satisfaction at the high-demand endpoint is 96% for FULL versus ≈55–58% for those three simpler patterns, a **≈38–41-percentage-point** difference, not an EE percentage. (B, §V.1–2, Figs.7–8.)
+
+There is an explicit **negative component marginal**: with five satellites, adding load balancing gives **92.2 versus 105.5 Mbps RT throughput, −12.6%**. The source explains that satellites are “almost all in an overloaded state”; distance-based association then “achieves better SINR and thus results in superior throughput.” At 25 satellites, adding interference coordination has a “minimal” RT effect because resources are abundant. Separately, load-utilization disparity at 15 satellites is 0.11 with balancing versus 0.72 for nearest association, about an 85% reduction in disparity, not an 85% throughput gain. (B, §V, Table III, pp.1716–1718.)
+
+**U — Joint uplink allocation (2025).** This contains the closest local-optimum-escape comparison. BBHMA-1 greedily swaps beams between hopping patterns until two-sided exchange stability; it “lacks the ability to escape from local optimum due to its greedy strategy.” BBHMA-2 uses simulated annealing to escape. These are **pairwise beam-pattern swaps**, not unilateral user moves. Its objective is a **weighted sum of terminal EEs**, not pooled network bits/joules. (U, §II-C, Eqs.(6)–(8), p.41251; §III-A, pp.41252–41254.)
+
+| Comparison in U | Magnitude and limitation |
+|---|---|
+| BBHMA-2 versus stable greedy BBHMA-1, both followed by JPFA power/frequency optimization | Fig.10 plateaus ≈46.25×10⁴ versus ≈45.7×10⁴ in total-period WSEE: **≈1.2%** extra. |
+| BBHMA-1+JPFA versus DRL-BH+JPFA / spatial-isolation SPBH-3D+JPFA | ≈45.7×10⁴ versus ≈45.5×10⁴ / ≈45.4×10⁴: only **≈0.4% / ≈0.7%**. |
+| Single-slot threshold comparison across hopping schemes | At WSEE ≥45,000, exceedance probabilities are approximately BBHMA-2 88%, BBHMA-1 78%, DRL-BH 66%, SPBH-3D 64%, SPBH-2D 46%, periodic 40%, random 22%. The annealing advantage is **10 percentage points of threshold probability**, not 10% more EE. |
+| Whole-period hopping comparison before common JPFA | BBHMA-1, SPBH-3D, and DRL-BH “do not show significant differences”; aggregation “obscures optimization at the time-slot level.” No exact scalar gap supplied. |
+| Joint frequency/power allocation versus fixed frequencies with optimized power | Fig.11 ≈28.9k versus ≈24.4k WSEE: ≈18%. This adds a resource variable; it is not an association-search premium. |
+| Same figure, alternative objectives | MaxGEE ≈19.2k and MaxCC ≈15.2k versus JPFA ≈28.9k: ≈51% and ≈90% in the evaluated WSEE. These comparators optimize different objectives. The interference-free upper reference is ≈29.1k. |
+
+Sources: U, §IV-A–D, pp.41257–41261, Figs.3–4, 10–11. The paper labels decreasing exceedance curves “C.D.F”; the table describes what is plotted. Its reported “34%” improvement over ignoring EE sensitivity appears normalized to the proposed value; it is not the ordinary baseline-denominator gain implied by Fig.11. Most tellingly, subsequent experiments use **the cheaper BBHMA-1**, “because the complexity of BBHMA-1 is less than BBHMA-2.” This paper offers no basis for demanding a large residual above the stable greedy solution. (U, §IV-D, pp.41260–41261.)
+
+**D — Dual-agent handover and power allocation (2026).** “Joint” here means DQN handover coupled to PPO power allocation: “The output of the DQN agent serves as the input for the PPO agent.” Its evaluation concerns average rate and timeout counts, not pooled EE. (D, §§II-C–III, p.3.)
+
+| Comparator | Average-rate comparison | Timeout comparison |
+|---|---|---|
+| GW: urgency-greedy scheduling plus water filling | At 60 W, ≈370 versus ≈165 Mbps: ≈124%; at 220 W, ≈440 versus ≈235: ≈87% | At 100 users, ≈32 versus ≈65 timed-out users: ≈51% fewer |
+| PW: rotating polling plus water filling | At 60 W, ≈370 versus ≈320 Mbps; at 220 W, ≈440 versus ≈380: ≈16% at both endpoints | At 100 users, ≈32 versus ≈57: ≈44% fewer |
+| DQN+DDPG: another coordinated dual-agent system | At 220 W, ≈440 versus ≈420 Mbps: ≈5%; training reward reported 350 versus 300 | Learning curves reach those rewards after ≈120 versus ≈170 episodes; this is training convergence |
+| DQN+random power / PPO+random handover | At 220 W, ≈440 versus ≈340 / ≈300 Mbps: ≈29% / ≈47% | These remove informed decisions for one variable; they are not competent iterative assignment baselines |
+
+Sources: D, §IV, pp.4–5, Figs.3–5. At 20 users timeout counts are approximately zero across methods. The source explains the larger load-dependent QoS gap: GW “repeatedly favors a small set of urgent users.” It also openly accepts a negative throughput marginal from a training signal: removing the timeout penalty gives **higher average rate**, while including it reduces timeouts. The reward is a “suboptimal compromise rather than a multi-objective global optimum.” That is a deliberate tradeoff, not proof that each reward component raises every headline metric. (D, §II-C, p.3; §IV, p.5.)
+
+**T — Terrestrial beam coordination (2017).** This fixes serving associations and optimizes beamforming/power. It distinguishes pooled network EE from weighted-sum cell EE. It is relevant evidence about interference and competent iterative endpoints, not a direct handover experiment. (T, §II, pp.6099–6101, Eqs.(10)–(11).)
+
+| Comparison in T | Magnitude and actual intervention |
+|---|---|
+| Proposed SCA versus iterative Dinkelbach/WMMSE ignoring rate-dependent processing power | “Up to 60%” pooled-EE gain with nonlinear processing cost; **no optimizer gain in the common linear-cost case**. This is correct objective modeling, not simultaneous association. |
+| Weighted-sum SCA versus prior parametric iterative method | At the high linear-cost endpoint, ≈89 versus ≈87 Mbit/J: ≈2%; nonlinear-cost gaps are larger. No single overall percentage is reported. |
+| Coordinated optimized beam directions versus fixed-direction multicell MMSE / single-cell MMSE | At 11 antennas, ≈15.0 versus ≈11.7 / ≈9.9 Mbit/J pooled EE: ≈28% / ≈52%. MMSE powers are iteratively optimized, but its directions are held fixed. |
+| Same coordinated method versus orthogonal / uncoordinated access | At 11 antennas, ≈15.0 versus ≈7.1 / ≈4.8 Mbit/J: ≈111% / ≈213%. Orthogonal access sacrifices bandwidth; uncoordinated access creates “severe inter-cell interference.” |
+| Distributed variants versus centralized alternating optimization | “All the variants … converge to the centralized solution.” Fig.8 shows essentially coincident endpoints; no residual percentage reported. |
+| EE-aware pilot grouping versus path-gain greedy pilot assignment | At 15 pilots, ≈15.6–15.7 versus ≈14 Mbit/J: ≈11–12%; at 20–21 pilots, nearly equal. The proposed assignment is itself heuristic/greedy. |
+
+Sources: T, §III-A Remark 2; §VII, pp.6107–6109, Figs.3–5, 8–9. The scenario deliberately has overlapping cell edges and users placed at those edges. Limited pilot resources make interference-aware grouping more valuable; with abundant pilots the conventional greedy method is “only slightly worse.” At the smallest plotted pilot count both methods are poor, so scarcity is not a monotonic guarantee of a larger gap. (T, §VII, pp.6106, 6109.)
+
+**C — CDRL versus iterative BPOF.** BPOF uses “alternating iterative decomposition plus a greedy post-processing step”; both methods jointly optimize beamforming and switching variables. This is not assignment versus unilateral association. At 20-dBm maximum power, the figures give CDRL/BPOF EE ≈46.6/45.6 for active RIS and ≈32.4/31.7 for passive RIS: about **2%** gains. Corresponding sum rates are ≈12.3/12.0 and ≈9.7/9.5, also about **2–3%**. The user-count, RIS-location, and antenna-count sweeps show similarly close curves, generally low-single-digit relative differences; the source gives no table of exact marginal percentages or convergence certificate for BPOF. It explicitly says “EE differences among passive RIS schemes are small.” All-on hardware comparisons change the allowed switching behavior and should not be used as evidence of an assignment-search premium. (C, §3.4, Table 3.1, pp.22–23; §5.2, Figs.5.1–5.6, pp.42–50.)
+
+**A — Multibeam resource allocation (2026).** Refined P-center clustering itself iteratively reassigns users until no radius reduction remains. Against K-means/K-means++, the text says it achieves **98.6–98.9% of K-means++ throughput**, while beating K-means, “with faster convergence, aligning with LEO’s real-time demands.” However, Fig.3 draws refined P-center slightly above K-means++, contradicting that direction: treat the 1.1–1.4% sacrifice as an author statement, not a reconciled measurement. Against Max-Dur, Min-Dis, and Max-SNR association heuristics, it reports **66.7–76% fewer hopping cycles** for uniform users and **70.2–89.7% fewer** for nonuniform users at 1,000 users, retaining over 92% coverage. It does not assign every range endpoint to an individual comparator. Hopping cycles are service rounds, not CPU time or EE. The PPO section shows training reward/SINR curves, not an additional converged-unilateral comparison. (A, §III-A, pp.173–174; §IV-B–C, pp.176–177, Figs.3–4.)
+
+**H — Handover and beam switching, HOBS (2024).** HOBS restricts physical beam scanning using history; HOBS-P also adjusts power, while HOBS-F uses full power. EBS exhaustively scans beams and uses full power. The source reports throughput approximately **1.2–2× EBS**. At a three-second frame, Fig.6 gives HOBS-P/EBS EE ≈3.0/2.2×10⁷ bit/J at low user density and ≈4.0/3.6×10⁷ at higher density: ≈36% and ≈11%. HOBS-F’s EE is close to EBS. These gains combine acquisition overhead and power control; “exhaustive” means physical beam sweeping, not exhaustive joint multi-user search. (H, §§IV–V, PDF pp.3–7, Figs.4–6; EBS “always scans all beams … but induces unaffordable training overhead.”)
+
+**When is coordination worth more? What this corpus actually identifies**
+
+| Candidate condition | Supported conclusion | Boundary of the evidence |
+|---|---|---|
+| Uneven load and available alternative capacity | Load-aware assignment can strongly outperform geometry/SINR association when some cells overload and others are underused. | Y, §§I-A, VI-A–B; B, §V. If all satellites overload, balancing can hurt: B’s −12.6% example. “More load” alone is insufficient. |
+| High offered demand, packet deadlines, conflicting assignments | B’s local-pattern gains grow after demand becomes challenging; D’s urgency-only greedy heuristic produces many more timeouts; R prevents task conflicts and battery exhaustion. | B, §V.1; D, §IV; R, Empirical Experiments. These are different omissions in the baselines, not proof of a large same-objective unilateral residual. |
+| Interference coupling and scarce shared resources | T shows large coordinated-versus-uncoordinated beamforming gains; pilot grouping matters more when useful orthogonal resources are limited. B and U coordinate simultaneous interfering beams. | T, §VII, Figs.5, 9; B, §§IV–V; U, §III-A. Competent coordinated iterative procedures can still approach the same endpoint. |
+| Cell/beam overlap and elevation | The channel papers directly link lower elevation and overlapping main lobes to more interference. | E, §III: “the influence of neighboring beam interference decreases” as elevation rises. S, §V: overlapping main lobes drive interference up. **Neither measures a coordination gain.** S also finds heavy shadowing can make the system “fully noise-limited.” |
+| Power constraints | Battery depletion is decisive in R; D sweeps RF power; T finds processing-power modeling more valuable at low transmit power; U finds power-sweep differences mainly determined by frequency allocation. | These are distinct mechanisms. No source isolates a binding RF cap as a general cause of a large simultaneous-association premium over converged unilateral search. |
+| Users per beam | Congestion/interference are relevant, but there is no controlled sweep of the project’s joint-minus-certified-unilateral EE gap against beam occupancy. | D fixes at most two users per satellite; U’s baseline uses four terminals per beam with FDMA; E fixes ten users per beam. None establishes a threshold for the project’s equal-airtime/discrete-MCS controller. |
+
+**The blunt answer is not “the literature guarantees a large coordination gain.”** The closest local-search evidence is modest: U’s ≈1.2% benefit from annealing beyond exchange-stable greedy matching; Y’s nearly overlapping optimized associations; T’s common centralized/distributed endpoint. B even supplies a ≈0.2–0.3% marginal coordination regime. But the corpus also does **not** establish the universal proposition that joint gains are generally small after any good unilateral algorithm converges. Those algorithms, move neighborhoods, metrics, and environments differ. The justified statement is narrower: **large residual coordination gains are not established here, and several relevant examples warn against assuming them.** (Y, §VI-B; T, §VII; U, §IV-D; B, §V.2, quoted above.)
+
+For this project, the supplied **+0.51%** measures the residual benefit of the tested bounded joint search over the certified unilateral fixed point. The **+442%** measures the benefit of that load/objective-aware unilateral optimizer over geometry-only association. They answer different questions. Neither measures the causal contribution of any learned component; neither establishes a global joint optimum. A bounded search’s observed advantage is not an upper bound on every possible coordinated policy. These are logical limits of the supplied measurements, not additional literature results.

